<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    JWT/JWS verification, compact serialization (header.payload.signature).
    Runs when signature.verify.type = "jwt" AND
    signature.verify.jwt.detached != "true" (see the -Detached sibling
    for that case).

    Native VerifyJWS — no custom crypto needed, unlike Cavage/XML-DSig.
    KeyStore/Alias/KnownHeaders are all per-proxy, resolved at runtime
    from this proxy's KVM config (see EV-JWT-Verify-Parse-Config) via
    ref — the same ref-to-a-flow-variable pattern
    ServiceCallout/TargetEndpoint use for <SSLInfo><KeyStore ref="...">.
    KnownHeaders is this proxy's RFC 7515 §4.1.11 "crit" allow-list,
    the same list the signing side's JOSE header template uses for its
    own "crit" array (see
    examples/kvm/orders-api.signature.jwt-header.json) — kept in sync
    manually since they're two separate KVM entries with different
    value shapes (a JSON array there, a comma-separated string here;
    see EV-JWT-Verify-Parse-Config for why).

    continueOnError defaults to false, so an invalid/expired/malformed
    JWS raises a policy fault here and the request is rejected; nothing
    else needs to check a "valid" flag. Add a FaultRule if you want a
    response shape other than Apigee's default JWS-fault response.

    Algorithm stays pinned here rather than KVM-sourced: it's the one
    piece where letting anything outside a reviewed policy change pick
    the accepted value opens an algorithm-confusion downgrade attack.
    If more than one algorithm is genuinely needed, add more
    <Algorithm> entries here — don't make it fully dynamic.
    IgnoreCriticalHeaders also stays a fixed "false": accepting a JWS
    whose "crit" lists something outside KnownHeaders should fail
    closed, not be toggle-able by config.

    UNVERIFIED: I can't check the exact <KnownHeaders ref="..."> shape
    (a single ref to a delimited string vs. something else) against
    Apigee's reference docs from here — if it turns out to need a
    different structure, adjust this policy; nothing else in the
    shared flow depends on its internals.
-->
<VerifyJWS name="VerifyJWS-Jwt-Compact" continueOnError="false">
    <DisplayName>VerifyJWS-Jwt-Compact</DisplayName>
    <Algorithm>RS256</Algorithm>
    <Source>signature.verify.jwt.jws</Source>
    <PublicKey>
        <KeyStore ref="signature.verify.jwt.keystore.name"/>
        <Alias ref="signature.verify.jwt.keystore.alias"/>
    </PublicKey>
    <KnownHeaders ref="signature.verify.jwt.knownHeaders"/>
    <IgnoreCriticalHeaders>false</IgnoreCriticalHeaders>
    <VariablePrefix>signature.verify.jwt</VariablePrefix>
</VerifyJWS>
